WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.940
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:03.940 --> 00:00:06.287
Building the Attribute-Driven Migration.

3
00:00:06.287 --> 00:00:09.362
This is Chapter 4: dynamic rules as code.

4
00:00:09.362 --> 00:00:13.413
A dynamic membership rule is
access policy written as one line,

5
00:00:13.413 --> 00:00:16.216
so the chapter treats
it like code: reviewed,

6
00:00:16.216 --> 00:00:20.188
tested against people you can
name, and diffed before it ships.

7
00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:25.210
We'll look at what Microsoft's engine and
samples give you, what they leave out,

8
00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.398
then the rule library, the
linter, the shadow diff,

9
00:00:28.398 --> 00:00:31.188
the incident path, two
labs and the dangers.

10
00:00:31.188 --> 00:00:36.710
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

11
00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:42.697
Why treat a one-line rule as code?

12
00:00:42.697 --> 00:00:46.810
Because in this migration,
access follows the HR record.

13
00:00:46.810 --> 00:00:49.685
HR values land in a few coded attributes,

14
00:00:49.685 --> 00:00:54.998
and dynamic rules read them to decide
who gets access, licenses and policy.

15
00:00:54.998 --> 00:00:58.615
Every rule that grants access
starts with two guards:

16
00:00:58.615 --> 00:01:01.985
the account is enabled, and
it's a member, not a guest.

17
00:01:01.985 --> 00:01:06.635
The validation kit from Chapter 3 already
checks the rules live in a tenant.

18
00:01:06.635 --> 00:01:10.302
This chapter moves those checks
earlier, into the pull request,

19
00:01:10.302 --> 00:01:14.260
so a bad rule is caught before
the engine starts moving people.

20
00:01:18.010 --> 00:01:21.510
What Learn says about the engine is
what makes this worth the trouble.

21
00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:26.185
The rule language is small and precise,
so a parser can follow it exactly.

22
00:01:26.185 --> 00:01:30.155
Comparisons ignore case, so a
lower-case code still matches,

23
00:01:30.155 --> 00:01:32.910
and the HR side has to
keep its codes clean.

24
00:01:32.910 --> 00:01:37.001
Processing runs one tenant at a
time and can take more than a day,

25
00:01:37.001 --> 00:01:39.598
so a mistake doesn't undo itself quickly.

26
00:01:39.598 --> 00:01:42.987
The memberOf operator is a
preview that ends in November,

27
00:01:42.987 --> 00:01:44.935
after which those groups freeze.

28
00:01:44.935 --> 00:01:48.626
And agents' user accounts
are included unless excluded,

29
00:01:48.626 --> 00:01:52.200
with no documented property
to exclude them yet.

30
00:01:55.950 --> 00:01:58.537
Which cloud you're in
matters here in a quiet way.

31
00:01:58.537 --> 00:02:01.407
Learn's incident samples
ask you for the cloud,

32
00:02:01.407 --> 00:02:06.389
but if they don't recognize your answer,
they fall back to the worldwide endpoint,

33
00:02:06.389 --> 00:02:10.013
so a GCC High tenant could be
signed in to as the wrong cloud.

34
00:02:10.013 --> 00:02:14.688
The chapter's code uses the same hosts
but refuses a cloud name it doesn't know.

35
00:02:14.688 --> 00:02:18.765
Graph's own rule evaluator is
a beta, delegated-only action,

36
00:02:18.765 --> 00:02:22.926
listed for the global service
and the US Government clouds.

37
00:02:22.926 --> 00:02:25.697
And the memberOf preview
is public cloud only,

38
00:02:25.697 --> 00:02:29.060
which is one more reason
the library doesn't use it.

39
00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:34.460
So what does Microsoft give you?

40
00:02:34.460 --> 00:02:38.716
For writing rules: a syntax page
with every property and operator,

41
00:02:38.716 --> 00:02:41.160
and a page on writing efficient rules.

42
00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.026
There's no sample library
of rules or tests;

43
00:02:44.026 --> 00:02:47.548
the closest thing is Graph's
evaluator, which is beta.

44
00:02:47.548 --> 00:02:52.029
For running the engine, there's more:
five PowerShell samples for an incident,

45
00:02:52.029 --> 00:02:55.372
when a rule change starts moving
people you didn't intend.

46
00:02:55.372 --> 00:02:59.848
You pause processing, then resume
it in order, critical groups first.

47
00:02:59.848 --> 00:03:03.290
Each script works in two
phases, asks before it acts,

48
00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:06.520
and asks only for the
permissions it needs.

49
00:03:10.270 --> 00:03:13.156
The scripts only exist
inside Learn's pages,

50
00:03:13.156 --> 00:03:16.923
so the repository pins the
pages themselves, unmodified,

51
00:03:16.923 --> 00:03:21.733
at a known commit, with hashes a test
checks and a weekly watch for changes.

52
00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:25.686
Then a small harness pulls each
script out of its pinned page

53
00:03:25.686 --> 00:03:29.482
at test time and runs it, as
published, against a fake tenant.

54
00:03:29.482 --> 00:03:33.178
Only the things it calls
outside itself are swapped out:

55
00:03:33.178 --> 00:03:37.357
the prompts, the screen output,
the sleep, and four Graph cmdlets.

56
00:03:37.357 --> 00:03:40.477
That way you can watch
Microsoft's own scripts behave,

57
00:03:40.477 --> 00:03:42.320
instead of taking them on trust.

58
00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:43.950
Here's what that showed.

59
00:03:47.700 --> 00:03:49.462
This is the heart of the chapter.

60
00:03:49.462 --> 00:03:53.975
Learn's pause script does its job well,
and the code keeps it as the model.

61
00:03:53.975 --> 00:03:56.325
But it records nothing
about what it paused.

62
00:03:56.325 --> 00:03:59.934
So when you resume, Learn's script
turns on every paused group,

63
00:03:59.934 --> 00:04:04.337
including one that was deliberately
frozen before the incident started.

64
00:04:04.337 --> 00:04:08.310
It also falls back to the worldwide
cloud on an answer it doesn't know,

65
00:04:08.310 --> 00:04:10.863
and nothing makes you
wait before resuming.

66
00:04:10.863 --> 00:04:12.650
The verdict: during recovery,

67
00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:16.300
the questions are what did we
pause, and what was already off.

68
00:04:16.300 --> 00:04:18.260
Only a record answers them.

69
00:04:22.010 --> 00:04:23.910
Here's what a rule looks like as code.

70
00:04:23.910 --> 00:04:28.487
Each group is one small file: a name,
whether it holds users or devices,

71
00:04:28.487 --> 00:04:33.880
an owner who approves changes, a flag saying
whether it's critical in an incident,

72
00:04:33.880 --> 00:04:37.885
the rule itself with its two guards
at the top, and a few tests.

73
00:04:37.885 --> 00:04:42.813
The tests name people in a fictional
population built to trip every guard:

74
00:04:42.813 --> 00:04:46.690
someone disabled, a guest who
copied an organization code,

75
00:04:46.690 --> 00:04:49.760
a worker with an empty
type, a code in lower case.

76
00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:54.060
If the rule ever lets the wrong
person in, a test says so.

77
00:04:57.810 --> 00:05:01.335
To test rules without a tenant,
the code needs to read them.

78
00:05:01.335 --> 00:05:06.123
The parser follows the grammar on
Learn's syntax page: the operators,

79
00:05:06.123 --> 00:05:10.997
lists, collections, nulls, escaped
quotes and the order of precedence.

80
00:05:10.997 --> 00:05:13.426
It even accepts the en dash that some of

81
00:05:13.426 --> 00:05:16.310
Learn's own examples
print instead of a hyphen.

82
00:05:16.310 --> 00:05:20.994
The evaluator then checks a parsed
rule against one fictional person,

83
00:05:20.994 --> 00:05:23.498
ignoring case, just as the engine does.

84
00:05:23.498 --> 00:05:28.648
It's honest about its limits: anything it
can't evaluate faithfully, it refuses.

85
00:05:28.648 --> 00:05:33.530
It's a test tool, not the engine,
and Lab 2 checks it against Graph's.

86
00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:40.017
The linter is where the
guards become enforceable.

87
00:05:40.017 --> 00:05:43.040
Its most important check
is that the two guards sit

88
00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:45.918
at the top of the rule,
ANDed with everything else.

89
00:05:45.918 --> 00:05:48.784
A guard tucked inside an
"or" protects nothing,

90
00:05:48.784 --> 00:05:52.968
yet a simple text search would still
find it and call the rule fine.

91
00:05:52.968 --> 00:05:56.356
That's why the linter works on
the parsed rule, not the text.

92
00:05:56.356 --> 00:05:59.680
Another check is subtler:
"not equal to contractor"

93
00:05:59.680 --> 00:06:02.842
also lets in someone whose
worker type is empty.

94
00:06:02.842 --> 00:06:05.970
The linter flags it,
and the owner decides.

95
00:06:09.720 --> 00:06:13.445
The best proof a linter is useful
is that it finds something real.

96
00:06:13.445 --> 00:06:18.320
Run against Book 3's own table of
rule patterns, it found three things.

97
00:06:18.320 --> 00:06:21.796
One pattern had no guest guard,
so a guest with a matching

98
00:06:21.796 --> 00:06:25.045
employer name could have
qualified; that's now fixed.

99
00:06:25.045 --> 00:06:27.536
Another let in a worker
with an empty type,

100
00:06:27.536 --> 00:06:31.345
and the owner decided that shouldn't
count, so it gained a clause.

101
00:06:31.345 --> 00:06:34.481
And the direct reports rule
can't carry guards at all,

102
00:06:34.481 --> 00:06:39.660
because Learn says it can't be combined
with anything, so the linter just notes it.

103
00:06:43.410 --> 00:06:45.385
Two more pieces finish the code.

104
00:06:45.385 --> 00:06:50.391
The shadow diff runs the old rule and
the new one over the fictional population

105
00:06:50.391 --> 00:06:55.397
and lists exactly who a change adds and
who it removes, right in the pull request.

106
00:06:55.397 --> 00:06:58.560
The same idea runs live
in the validation kit.

107
00:06:58.560 --> 00:07:02.286
The pause manifest is the record
Learn's samples leave out:

108
00:07:02.286 --> 00:07:06.168
before anything is paused, it
writes down every group's state,

109
00:07:06.168 --> 00:07:10.360
then pauses only what was on, and
later resumes only what it paused.

110
00:07:10.360 --> 00:07:14.880
Seventy tests cover all of it,
with no tenant and no network.

111
00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:19.692
Who is this for?

112
00:07:19.692 --> 00:07:23.393
Developers build and maintain
the library and its checks.

113
00:07:23.393 --> 00:07:26.099
Identity engineers own the group library,

114
00:07:26.099 --> 00:07:29.967
and every rule file names the
owner who approves its changes.

115
00:07:29.967 --> 00:07:34.204
Reviewers approve each change, with
the shadow diff in front of them.

116
00:07:34.204 --> 00:07:36.805
And each step asks for the least it needs.

117
00:07:36.805 --> 00:07:41.092
Creating groups needs group write rights
with the Groups Administrator role.

118
00:07:41.092 --> 00:07:45.579
Testing a rule with Graph needs only
read rights when you supply the rule.

119
00:07:45.579 --> 00:07:48.504
Pausing administrative
units is the heavier one:

120
00:07:48.504 --> 00:07:51.500
it needs the Privileged
Role Administrator.

121
00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:59.388
Lab 1 needs only a laptop
with PowerShell 7 and Pester.

122
00:07:59.388 --> 00:08:03.176
First you run Learn's own pause
and resume scripts against a fake

123
00:08:03.176 --> 00:08:06.750
tenant with two groups, one of
them already paused for review.

124
00:08:06.750 --> 00:08:11.075
After the resume, both are on:
the held group came back to life.

125
00:08:11.075 --> 00:08:15.250
Try an unknown cloud name and
you'll see it sign in to worldwide.

126
00:08:15.250 --> 00:08:20.125
Then you lint Book 3's table and evaluate
a rule over the fictional people.

127
00:08:20.125 --> 00:08:22.866
The disabled account
and the guest stay out,

128
00:08:22.866 --> 00:08:26.270
and the lower-case code gets
in, just as Learn says.

129
00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:32.382
Now the part that makes
the case for all this.

130
00:08:32.382 --> 00:08:37.608
Run the pull request check and it
passes cleanly: six rules, no findings.

131
00:08:37.608 --> 00:08:40.524
Then break a rule on purpose
by deleting the clause

132
00:08:40.524 --> 00:08:43.371
that keeps out empty worker
types, and run it again.

133
00:08:43.371 --> 00:08:45.070
Three things fire at once.

134
00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:49.604
The linter flags the empty-slot risk,
the membership test for the worker with

135
00:08:49.604 --> 00:08:53.995
no type fails, and the shadow diff shows
exactly who the change would let in.

136
00:08:53.995 --> 00:08:58.640
Put the clause back, run the whole
suite, and seventy tests pass.

137
00:09:02.390 --> 00:09:05.375
Lab 2 moves to a test
tenant, never production,

138
00:09:05.375 --> 00:09:08.603
with fictional users that
match the population.

139
00:09:08.603 --> 00:09:13.640
You create the groups from the library,
paused, so they can't move anyone yet.

140
00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:17.623
Then the key step: ask Graph's own
evaluator about every test user,

141
00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:20.328
and compare its answer
with the offline one.

142
00:09:20.328 --> 00:09:24.228
Any disagreement is a finding
against the offline evaluator.

143
00:09:24.228 --> 00:09:27.552
Next you turn processing on
and diff two rules live.

144
00:09:27.552 --> 00:09:31.205
Finally you drill an incident:
take the record, pause,

145
00:09:31.205 --> 00:09:35.810
resume the critical groups by
name, then back everything out.

146
00:09:39.560 --> 00:09:42.522
Each of these dangers has a
guard, and most of them are

147
00:09:42.522 --> 00:09:45.898
rules that run every time rather
than someone remembering.

148
00:09:45.898 --> 00:09:49.233
The quietest one is a guard
hidden inside an "or":

149
00:09:49.233 --> 00:09:53.973
the rule looks right, a text search
agrees, and guests get in anyway.

150
00:09:53.973 --> 00:09:57.769
The loudest is a change that moves
hundreds of people within hours,

151
00:09:57.769 --> 00:10:00.598
which is why every change
carries tests and a diff.

152
00:10:00.598 --> 00:10:04.297
After an incident, resuming
everything is its own danger.

153
00:10:04.297 --> 00:10:10.220
And one row stays honestly open: agents'
accounts, until Learn names the property.

154
00:10:13.970 --> 00:10:15.320
Here's what done looks like.

155
00:10:15.320 --> 00:10:19.795
Every group has a file with an owner and
tests for someone in and someone out.

156
00:10:19.795 --> 00:10:23.945
The pull request check passes, with
its diff in front of the reviewer.

157
00:10:23.945 --> 00:10:29.040
Every rule starts with the two guards,
avoids memberOf and display-name attributes,

158
00:10:29.040 --> 00:10:31.920
and makes a written
decision about empty slots.

159
00:10:31.920 --> 00:10:35.820
The offline evaluator agrees
with Graph's for every test.

160
00:10:35.820 --> 00:10:40.357
And the incident runbook takes its record
before pausing and resumes from it.

161
00:10:40.357 --> 00:10:44.783
Next, Chapter 5 builds Lifecycle
Workflows custom extensions.

162
00:10:44.783 --> 00:10:45.940
Thanks for watching.
